1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,344
Your CTO asks: "Two engineers ran git
pull on the same diverged branch.

2
00:00:09,384 --> 00:00:13,816
One got a merge commit, one got a
rebase, the third got an error.

3
00:00:13,856 --> 00:00:16,018
Same repository, same Git. Why?"

4
00:00:16,358 --> 00:00:20,882
Same repository, same version of
Git, same command, three outcomes.

5
00:00:20,922 --> 00:00:24,015
How can that be? Say your
best guess out loud.

6
00:00:28,855 --> 00:00:32,459
Engineer A has pull.rebase=true
in her personal file.

7
00:00:32,499 --> 00:00:37,960
Engineer B has nothing. Engineer C's clone
has pull.rebase=false in .git/config,

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,392
left by an old setup script. Git assembles
its behavior from several files, the

9
00:00:43,432 --> 00:00:48,686
command line and the environment, and
none of it travels with the repository.

10
00:00:48,726 --> 00:00:54,393
One command per machine settles the question,
and you'll know it in twenty minutes.

11
00:00:54,433 --> 00:00:57,899
Keep those three engineers
in mind. They come back.

12
00:00:59,739 --> 00:01:03,579
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:01:03,619 --> 00:01:08,476
The last module of Part 1 is configuration:
the settings that tell Git how to

14
00:01:08,516 --> 00:01:13,755
behave. You've been reading configuration
listings since video 1, where the sandbox

15
00:01:13,795 --> 00:01:18,016
showed its global and local lines.
Now we take the mechanism apart.

16
00:01:18,356 --> 00:01:22,971
One idea from the textbook runs through
this chapter: Git records what it is told.

17
00:01:23,011 --> 00:01:27,398
Configuration decides what Git does on
your machine, and is never transferred.

18
00:01:27,738 --> 00:01:32,209
Four things today. The scopes and their
order. The command that shows where every

19
00:01:32,249 --> 00:01:36,830
value comes from. The subcommands for reading
and writing, and the Git version they

20
00:01:36,870 --> 00:01:40,505
need. And what happens with an
unknown key and with a broken file.

21
00:01:42,345 --> 00:01:43,924
Here's what you'll walk away with.

22
00:01:44,264 --> 00:01:45,676
After this video you can:

23
00:01:46,016 --> 00:01:50,820
Name the configuration scopes, and say
which value wins when several set one key.

24
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:54,998
Show the origin and scope of every
setting in force with one command.

25
00:01:55,338 --> 00:02:00,460
Read, set and unset a key with the git config
subcommands, and state the Git version

26
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:01,074
they need.

27
00:02:01,414 --> 00:02:04,225
Explain what an unknown
key and a broken file do.

28
00:02:06,065 --> 00:02:10,972
In one sentence. A Git setting is a key
with one or more values, read from several

29
00:02:11,012 --> 00:02:16,040
files plus the command line, and when the
same key appears more than once, the value

30
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:19,358
read last wins. Each of those
places is called a scope.

31
00:02:19,698 --> 00:02:21,787
Five scopes, in reading order.

32
00:02:22,127 --> 00:02:26,671
system: the file etc/gitconfig under
the directory Git was installed in.

33
00:02:26,711 --> 00:02:32,020
Written with --system. Defaults an administrator
sets for every user of the machine.

34
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:37,241
Because it depends on the installation
directory, the Homebrew Git and Apple's Git

35
00:02:37,281 --> 00:02:39,788
on your Mac have different system files.

36
00:02:40,128 --> 00:02:44,817
global: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/config,
and then ~/.gitconfig.

37
00:02:44,857 --> 00:02:49,948
Written with --global. Your
identity, your editor, your aliases.

38
00:02:49,988 --> 00:02:55,398
"Global" means "for this user". It's
a smaller globe than it sounds.

39
00:02:55,738 --> 00:03:00,940
local: .git/config of the repository.
This is the default for writing.

40
00:03:00,980 --> 00:03:04,684
Remotes, upstreams,
repository-specific overrides.

41
00:03:05,024 --> 00:03:10,942
worktree: .git/config.worktree.
Settings for one working tree, only if

42
00:03:10,982 --> 00:03:13,751
extensions.worktreeConfig is set.

43
00:03:14,091 --> 00:03:20,573
command: git -c key=value, and the
GIT_CONFIG_COUNT family of environment variables.

44
00:03:20,613 --> 00:03:22,669
Not stored. One invocation.

45
00:03:23,009 --> 00:03:27,271
Read first to read last: system,
global, local, worktree, command.

46
00:03:27,311 --> 00:03:32,615
For a single-valued key, the value read
last wins. For a multi-valued key, nothing

47
00:03:32,655 --> 00:03:35,873
is overridden: all values
from all files are used.

48
00:03:36,213 --> 00:03:42,548
Quick quiz. A key is true in global and
false in local, and you pass a third value

49
00:03:42,588 --> 00:03:46,513
with -c on the command line.
Which wins? A, global.

50
00:03:46,553 --> 00:03:49,856
B, local. C, the command
line. Your answer?

51
00:03:54,696 --> 00:03:59,293
C. The command scope is read last, so it
wins. You'll watch it happen in the demo.

52
00:03:59,633 --> 00:04:03,994
Inside .git. Only config and,
optionally, config.worktree live there.

53
00:04:04,034 --> 00:04:09,095
Configuration is not an object and not a
ref: it has no history, and no transfer

54
00:04:09,135 --> 00:04:14,388
command carries it. A fresh clone has the
local configuration that git clone wrote,

55
00:04:14,428 --> 00:04:18,470
and nothing of the configuration
of the repository it came from.

56
00:04:18,810 --> 00:04:22,443
The environment sits beside
this stack, not inside it.

57
00:04:22,483 --> 00:04:27,886
Several things that look like configuration
are decided by environment variables

58
00:04:27,926 --> 00:04:33,192
first. An environment variable is a named
value that your shell hands to every

59
00:04:33,232 --> 00:04:37,818
program it starts. GIT_EDITOR beats
core.editor, which beats EDITOR.

60
00:04:37,858 --> 00:04:42,580
GIT_AUTHOR_NAME beats user.name, even
when user.name is given with -c.

61
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:47,274
git config list can't show these
overrides. git var shows the result.

62
00:04:47,614 --> 00:04:52,858
Protected configuration. The system, global
and command scopes are "protected": a

63
00:04:52,898 --> 00:04:58,012
few security-sensitive keys, safe.directory
among them, are honored only there.

64
00:04:58,052 --> 00:05:03,230
The local file belongs to whoever created
the repository directory, which is not

65
00:05:03,270 --> 00:05:08,384
always you: a repository unpacked from
an archive brings its .git/config along.

66
00:05:08,724 --> 00:05:14,521
The one command. git config list --show-scope
--show-origin prints three columns:

67
00:05:14,561 --> 00:05:20,502
scope, origin, and key with value. It's the
first thing to run whenever Git behaves

68
00:05:20,542 --> 00:05:25,907
differently on two machines. For one
key, git config get --all --show-scope

69
00:05:25,947 --> 00:05:31,744
--show-origin <key> prints every value in
reading order, and the last line is the

70
00:05:31,784 --> 00:05:32,249
winner.

71
00:05:32,589 --> 00:05:37,928
Reading and writing. Since Git 2.46, git
config has subcommands, and the older

72
00:05:37,968 --> 00:05:42,342
option forms are documented as
deprecated modes that still work.

73
00:05:42,382 --> 00:05:47,997
Write the new form. Read the old one,
because scripts and most tutorials still use

74
00:05:48,037 --> 00:05:48,204
it.

75
00:05:48,544 --> 00:05:55,257
Read one value: git config get <key>. The
legacy form is git config --get <key>.

76
00:05:55,297 --> 00:06:01,925
Read all values: get --all. Search by
pattern: get --all --show-names --regexp.

77
00:06:01,965 --> 00:06:07,242
Write: git config set <key>
<value>. Add a value: set --append.

78
00:06:07,282 --> 00:06:14,247
Remove: git config unset <key>. List: git
config list, with the legacy forms --list

79
00:06:14,287 --> 00:06:17,792
or -l. Open in an editor: git config edit.

80
00:06:18,132 --> 00:06:23,512
git config set, unset and edit are 🟡 CAUTION:
each changes one configuration file,

81
00:06:23,552 --> 00:06:28,800
which has no history. The preview is git
config get --show-origin <key>, and the

82
00:06:28,840 --> 00:06:31,377
recovery is to set the old value again.

83
00:06:31,717 --> 00:06:37,962
The exit status is part of the interface:
1 from get for a key that is not set, and

84
00:06:38,002 --> 00:06:44,171
5 from unset for a key that does not exist.
The subcommands don't exist before Git

85
00:06:44,211 --> 00:06:50,456
2.46. So a script that must run on older
installations either checks the version or

86
00:06:50,496 --> 00:06:56,589
uses the legacy spelling. The textbook
gives Ubuntu 24.04, which ships 2.43.0, as

87
00:06:56,629 --> 00:06:57,498
the example.

88
00:06:57,838 --> 00:07:02,676
Types and lists. Git stores text. A
type is applied when a value is read.

89
00:07:02,716 --> 00:07:07,955
2g is an integer with a scale suffix. A
path keeps its tilde in the file and is

90
00:07:07,995 --> 00:07:12,566
expanded on reading. A bare name
with no equals sign is boolean true.

91
00:07:12,606 --> 00:07:16,977
Some keys are lists, such as
remote.<name>.fetch and include.path.

92
00:07:17,017 --> 00:07:22,256
Plain get prints only the last value of
a list. set and unset refuse a key with

93
00:07:22,296 --> 00:07:24,595
several values until you say which.

94
00:07:24,935 --> 00:07:29,288
Unknown keys. git help --config
lists every variable this Git knows.

95
00:07:29,328 --> 00:07:34,716
Git doesn't check a key against that list.
A misspelled key is stored, reported, and

96
00:07:34,756 --> 00:07:38,464
ignored forever. Only a key
without a section is rejected.

97
00:07:38,504 --> 00:07:43,051
Other tools rely on this freedom to
keep their settings in Git's files.

98
00:07:43,091 --> 00:07:45,894
The cost is that a typing
mistake is silent.

99
00:07:46,234 --> 00:07:51,202
A broken file stops everything. Every
command reads the whole configuration before

100
00:07:51,242 --> 00:07:55,355
it does anything, and a parse error
in a file it must read is fatal.

101
00:07:57,195 --> 00:08:02,096
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy:
company policy, team convention, and what

102
00:08:02,136 --> 00:08:06,917
your manager tells you this morning. The
most specific instruction overrides the

103
00:08:06,957 --> 00:08:07,640
general one.

104
00:08:07,980 --> 00:08:13,185
Apply it to the hook. One engineer has a
personal habit, one has none, and one has a

105
00:08:13,225 --> 00:08:17,305
note pinned to that particular desk
by someone who left years ago.

106
00:08:17,345 --> 00:08:19,553
Same policy manual, three behaviors.

107
00:08:19,893 --> 00:08:24,811
The analogy breaks for multi-valued keys,
where nothing is overridden: every value

108
00:08:24,851 --> 00:08:28,802
from every file counts. And remember
what stands beside the stack.

109
00:08:28,842 --> 00:08:33,881
An environment variable is not in any of
these layers, and can overrule all of them.

110
00:08:35,721 --> 00:08:41,176
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper. Write
the five scopes from memory, in reading

111
00:08:41,216 --> 00:08:45,148
order, and circle the one that
wins. Then say them out loud.

112
00:08:49,988 --> 00:08:54,806
Left to right is reading order. The arrow
says it: whatever is read last wins.

113
00:08:54,846 --> 00:08:59,913
So the command line beats the repository,
the repository beats your personal file,

114
00:08:59,953 --> 00:09:04,709
and your personal file beats the machine.
The last line of the picture, about

115
00:09:04,749 --> 00:09:07,636
include.path, is the
subject of the next video.

116
00:09:09,476 --> 00:09:13,563
One safety note before we type.
The lab is a sealed room.

117
00:09:13,603 --> 00:09:19,646
Inside it, the global file is the sandbox's
home/.gitconfig, and the system scope is

118
00:09:19,686 --> 00:09:23,628
switched off, so your real
configuration is never read.

119
00:09:23,968 --> 00:09:29,554
In this chapter's demos the sandbox's home
directory is also HOME, so a tilde means

120
00:09:29,594 --> 00:09:30,707
the sandbox home.

121
00:09:31,047 --> 00:09:36,842
Three columns: scope, origin, key and value.
Five lines come from the global file.

122
00:09:36,882 --> 00:09:42,819
The core.* lines were written by git init.
Keys print in lower case, because section

123
00:09:42,859 --> 00:09:45,665
and variable names are case-insensitive.

124
00:09:46,005 --> 00:09:47,216
The files themselves.

125
00:09:47,556 --> 00:09:53,073
Now one key in two scopes. git config set
🟡 CAUTION: it rewrites a file that keeps

126
00:09:53,113 --> 00:09:56,529
no history. Global says
true, and local says false.

127
00:09:56,569 --> 00:09:59,307
Predict what get prints. Say it out loud.

128
00:10:04,147 --> 00:10:09,411
get prints the winning value, false. get
--all prints every value in reading order,

129
00:10:09,451 --> 00:10:11,456
and the last line is the winner.

130
00:10:11,796 --> 00:10:15,143
The command scope beats
both files, as in the quiz.

131
00:10:15,183 --> 00:10:20,523
The GIT_CONFIG_COUNT variables are the
same scope, for scripts that can't pass -c

132
00:10:20,563 --> 00:10:23,246
everywhere. And -c is read last and wins.

133
00:10:23,586 --> 00:10:29,914
The worktree scope has a trap: without
extensions.worktreeConfig, --worktree

134
00:10:29,954 --> 00:10:32,511
silently writes to .git/config.

135
00:10:32,851 --> 00:10:37,085
Without GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL, the
global scope has two files.

136
00:10:37,125 --> 00:10:42,318
~/.gitconfig is read after the XDG
file, and wins where both set a key.

137
00:10:42,358 --> 00:10:48,434
If you keep your configuration in the XDG
location, a stray ~/.gitconfig created by

138
00:10:48,474 --> 00:10:50,793
some tool silently overrides it.

139
00:10:51,133 --> 00:10:55,430
The system scope, shown with a
stand-in file inside the sandbox.

140
00:10:55,470 --> 00:11:01,055
The machine's real system file is not
touched. System says interactive, global says

141
00:11:01,095 --> 00:11:03,969
true, local says false.
Predict the winner.

142
00:11:08,809 --> 00:11:11,458
false wins: local is
read last of the three.

143
00:11:11,798 --> 00:11:14,672
Outside a repository,
there is no local scope.

144
00:11:15,012 --> 00:11:20,642
And the environment beside the stack:
core.editor is set, and git var GIT_EDITOR

145
00:11:20,682 --> 00:11:25,958
still prints true, because the lab sets
GIT_EDITOR so that no editor opens.

146
00:11:28,798 --> 00:11:31,358
The three subcommands,
with their exit statuses.

147
00:11:31,698 --> 00:11:35,906
The same operations with the flags
that older scripts and tutorials use.

148
00:11:35,946 --> 00:11:39,091
You see them once, so that
you can read those scripts.

149
00:11:39,431 --> 00:11:45,133
The file format: sections, optional
subsections in double quotes, and name = value

150
00:11:45,173 --> 00:11:50,805
lines. --comment, which the textbook dates
to Git 2.45, writes the reason next to

151
00:11:50,845 --> 00:11:51,505
the value.

152
00:11:59,845 --> 00:12:05,261
A list: plain get prints only the last
value, and get --all prints all of them.

153
00:12:16,901 --> 00:12:21,881
Which file would edit open for each scope?
An "editor" that only prints its argument

154
00:12:21,921 --> 00:12:22,478
tells you.

155
00:12:22,818 --> 00:12:26,845
Now a typing mistake: pull.rebsae.
Predict what Git says.

156
00:12:26,885 --> 00:12:27,987
Say it out loud.

157
00:12:32,827 --> 00:12:37,717
Nothing. The key is stored and reported,
and pull.rebase is still not set.

158
00:12:37,757 --> 00:12:42,048
That silence is why this mistake
is so common, and so forgivable.

159
00:12:42,388 --> 00:12:46,786
Last: a hand edit that leaves a section
header without its closing bracket.

160
00:12:46,826 --> 00:12:48,798
Predict which commands still work.

161
00:12:53,638 --> 00:12:58,534
None that must read that file. fatal: bad
config line, with the line number and the

162
00:12:58,574 --> 00:12:59,129
file name.

163
00:13:08,469 --> 00:13:10,117
Five mistakes to watch for.

164
00:13:10,457 --> 00:13:14,252
The same command behaves
differently on two machines.

165
00:13:14,292 --> 00:13:19,606
Root cause: a key is set in different
scopes on each; git config get --all

166
00:13:19,646 --> 00:13:24,381
--show-scope --show-origin <key>
shows every value and the winner.

167
00:13:24,721 --> 00:13:29,403
A setting "does not work". Root cause:
the key is misspelled; Git stores unknown

168
00:13:29,443 --> 00:13:31,232
keys and ignores them silently.

169
00:13:31,572 --> 00:13:37,156
core.editor is set and another editor
opens. Root cause: an environment variable

170
00:13:37,196 --> 00:13:42,640
such as GIT_EDITOR is read first; git
config list cannot show it, git var can.

171
00:13:42,980 --> 00:13:47,120
A script fails with an unknown
git config subcommand.

172
00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:51,853
Root cause: get, set, unset and
list need Git 2.46 or later.

173
00:13:52,193 --> 00:13:57,031
Every command fails with "bad config
line". Root cause: a hand edit broke the

174
00:13:57,071 --> 00:14:00,452
syntax, and a parse error in
a required file is fatal.

175
00:14:02,292 --> 00:14:06,268
Now, out of the lab, and
back to the three engineers.

176
00:14:06,308 --> 00:14:10,586
The CTO's question, resolved
as the textbook resolves it.

177
00:14:10,626 --> 00:14:15,586
Engineer A has pull.rebase=true in
~/.gitconfig: she got a rebase.

178
00:14:15,626 --> 00:14:20,965
Engineer B has nothing, so a diverged
git pull stops: he got the error.

179
00:14:21,005 --> 00:14:26,798
Engineer C's clone has pull.rebase=false
in .git/config, left by an old setup

180
00:14:26,838 --> 00:14:29,071
script: he got a merge commit.

181
00:14:29,411 --> 00:14:35,314
One command per machine settles it: git
config get --all --show-scope --show-origin

182
00:14:35,354 --> 00:14:40,542
pull.rebase. And the lesson for the
team: a setup script that writes into

183
00:14:40,582 --> 00:14:45,841
.git/config leaves behavior behind that
outlives the script, and overrides

184
00:14:45,881 --> 00:14:48,848
everybody's personal choice in that clone.

185
00:14:50,688 --> 00:14:56,827
Your turn. Do Lab 5.1, "Scopes and origins",
in lab-manual/m05-configuration.md.

186
00:14:56,867 --> 00:15:03,006
The lab changes global settings, so its
first step points GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL at a

187
00:15:03,046 --> 00:15:07,872
per-lab file inside the hands-on
sandbox. Please don't skip it.

188
00:15:08,212 --> 00:15:12,213
Before each get, predict the value
and the scope it will come from.

189
00:15:12,253 --> 00:15:15,168
Before get --all, predict
the order of the lines.

190
00:15:17,008 --> 00:15:21,653
Q10: "A setting is in the system, global
and local files with three different

191
00:15:21,693 --> 00:15:24,999
values, and an environment
variable also exists for it.

192
00:15:25,039 --> 00:15:27,920
Which wins, and which
single command shows you?"

193
00:15:30,260 --> 00:15:34,917
Answer out loud. A strong answer gives the
reading order and the rule, places the

194
00:15:34,957 --> 00:15:39,557
command line in that order, and then treats
the environment variable separately,

195
00:15:39,597 --> 00:15:44,370
because it's not part of the stack. Name
the command for the files, and the command

196
00:15:44,410 --> 00:15:47,908
that shows the final result when
the environment is involved.

197
00:15:49,748 --> 00:15:55,122
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: Git reads configuration from five

198
00:15:55,162 --> 00:15:58,999
scopes in order: system, global,
local, worktree, command.

199
00:15:59,039 --> 00:16:04,480
For a single-valued key the value read last
wins, and for a list all values count.

200
00:16:04,520 --> 00:16:08,824
Environment variables stand beside
the stack and can overrule it.

201
00:16:09,164 --> 00:16:14,739
git config list --show-scope --show-origin
shows where every setting comes from.

202
00:16:14,779 --> 00:16:20,353
I read, write and remove keys with git
config get, set and unset, which need Git

203
00:16:20,393 --> 00:16:26,178
2.46 or later. An unknown key is stored and
silently ignored, and a syntax error in

204
00:16:26,218 --> 00:16:31,512
a configuration file stops every command
until the file is repaired by hand.

205
00:16:33,352 --> 00:16:38,127
Read sections 14B.1 to
14B.3 of Chapter 14B.

206
00:16:38,467 --> 00:16:45,388
Do Exercises 5.1 to 5.3 (Level 1, from
"one key in the local file" to "typed

207
00:16:45,428 --> 00:16:49,509
values") in
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

208
00:16:49,849 --> 00:16:56,526
Challenge: Exercise 5.4, Level 2, "four
